WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.490
Swiss engineering has a reputation for being
precise and reliable.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.870
And the country has a reputation for being...
not paranoid, exactly,

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.599
but at least taking a lot of precautions about
their safety.

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:12.519
Switzerland is the only country in the world

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:16.059
with enough nuclear fallout shelters for the
entire population.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:19.160
Even now, it's a legal requirement that any
new build house here

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.310
must have a shelter if it's not close enough
to an existing one.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:26.550
And okay, a lot of those shelters are being
used mostly for storage these days,

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.240
probably for cheese, but if the world takes
a dark path?

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.460
Switzerland is ready.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.110
And that extends to this.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.180
The drinking water.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.500
I'm in Zurich, the largest city in Switzerland,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.060
and it has an entire backup water supply system.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.320
The city has hundreds of drinking fountains,
free for public use,

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.829
of all sorts of designs.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:49.110
And okay, most of them are fed from the same
pipes as homes and offices:

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.800
it's mostly filtered water from nearby Lake
Zurich,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.910
with a bit of spring water and ground water
mixed in.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.670
It is, of course, some of the safest drinking
water in the world.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:00.370
Because Switzerland.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.829
But in the event of an emergency:

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:04.010
let's say the lake was contaminated somehow,

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.130
or there's a days-long power cut that shuts
all the pumps down:

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.729
not that that's likely, because the electricity
supply is just as secure,

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:14.930
but if it happens: Zurich is still prepared.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.890
There is an entire backup water system,

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.960
fed by nothing more than gravity from a spring
on a nearby mountain.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.840
It runs through 150 kilometres of pipes to
taps in hospitals and fallout shelters,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.770
and special drinking fountains like this.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.800
These can all be adapted so they can fill
a lot of buckets

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.720
in a very short amount of time

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.409
with nothing more than some hoses and scaffolding.

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:37.409
This water isn't being wasted, by the way.

00:01:37.409 --> 00:01:38.600
I mean, it is, no-one's drinking it,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.450
but if it wasn't going through these pipes

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:45.590
it'd just be joining a river and heading straight
to the ocean by a different path.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.689
Lots of cities maintain some level of redundant
water supply:

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:52.829
some backup pipes, or emergency tankers in
case everything fails.

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:53.969
But Zurich?

00:01:53.969 --> 00:01:57.240
They have two systems, constantly tested,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.450
always running together.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:02.049
Just in case the world ends.

